`2eeff48f` was the **first run of the SQL tier at any SHA**. Every fast-tier receipt above it records that
tier as unmeasured; it no longer is. Its one failure was a declared expected-red
(`PendingMigration=MIG-21`), not a regression — the `RUN.md` beside it says why, and a red receipt is still
a receipt.

`23f6bbeb` is the first SHA where **both tiers ran at the same commit**, and the first with no red in
either: MIG-21 landed, so the expected-red is now a passing guard and its trait is gone. Note that a tier is
named in the table because the two are not interchangeable: a fast-tier green says nothing about the
triggers, filtered indexes and races that only exist on a real SQL Server catalog.

`f8b3a30f` and `23f6bbeb` were measured on two lanes that had not yet met; the rows are both true and
neither supersedes the other. `1da15fb1` is where they meet — the Training W3 lane merged the migration
author's chain tip before authoring on it, so it is the first SHA whose SQL tier covers both `23f6bbeb`'s
population and this branch's.

The rows above interleave two sets of runs measured on branches that had not met until the migration stack
was landed: some come from the integration branch, the rest from the migration chain that ran eight links
ahead of it. They are ordered by the wall-clock time of the SHA each ran against, and every one of them is
still true of the commit it names — **none supersedes another merely by sitting below it**, and none was
measured at a SHA that contains the other set. The merge kept both sets in full: taking either side would
have deleted true receipts, including every SQL-tier run that exists anywhere. Note the consequence for any
`RUN.md` written before the stack landed — `bb82b3a0`'s says the SQL tier "still has no run recorded against
any SHA", which was true of what its branch could see and is false of this table. A receipt is evidence
about its own SHA, not about the repository.

The full receipt for each run — command, environment, what was excluded and what is still unmeasured —
is the `RUN.md` in the directory named for its SHA.

`ffb29e4e` is a LANE commit, not an ancestor of any receipt above it: it ran on
`lane/growth-mail-postmark` off `349d8806`, which is why its count sits below the row before it.

`13217cfd` is the first row here whose SQL tier is **unrunnable rather than unrun**, and the distinction is
the whole reason it has a row at all. The tier was started twice on this branch: the first run found one
genuine failure (a second exact-full-Margin-set assertion the fast tier cannot see, fixed in this SHA), and
the second died with every remaining test reporting `Connection was terminated` after Docker's VM remounted
its filesystem read-only — the host volume is at 97 % and the VM's sparse disk image had grown until there
was nothing left. That is an estate-level incident, not a property of the commit, and **every
Testcontainers tier run on this host will keep inflating that image until somebody reclaims it.** The
`RUN.md` beside this row names exactly which tests are still owed and what they would have proven.

`50b85657` **supersedes that row**: the owner authorised a Docker recovery, and the same work then ran both
tiers at one SHA on a healthy host — **568/568 on the SQL tier**, 11 added and 0 removed, every outcome read
individually from the `.trx` rather than inferred from the summary. It is the first SHA at which MIG-23's
`THROW 50062` trigger, its `date` column and its deliberately non-unique read index exist on a chain-built
database, and the first at which the drop-the-trigger falsification has actually been executed. The
`13217cfd` row stays: a superseded run is still the honest record of what was true that hour.

`1de06906` is the SHA where the **migration stack meets the integration branch** — the end of a seven-link
landing, with the fast tier run at every one of the seven merge commits and not only at the last. Its
`RUN.md` carries the per-link table, the `has-pending-model-changes` result that proves the auto-merged
model snapshot did not fork, and the analysis of the one red: a **pre-existing non-deterministic test**
(`EventsOutboxDeliveryTests.The_message_carries_the_link_and_no_other_guest_data`) whose
`Assert.DoesNotContain("250", body)` matched inside a random GUID in the proposal link rather than on a
leaked amount. The failing `.trx` is kept beside the receipt as `…-FLAKE.trx`; that test fires on the order
of once per ~130 runs and will keep failing merges until the absence assertions are run against the body
with the link removed.

## The rule this directory keeps

A run recorded here was produced from a **clean checkout of one commit**, not from a working tree. The
run above was executed in a throwaway `git worktree` detached at the SHA, which is why the paths inside
the `.trx` read `wt-be-receipt` rather than the main checkout: a suite run in a shared worktree while
sibling lanes are editing it measures no commit at all, and that is the exact defect this directory
exists to stop.

Adding a run means: run it at a SHA, drop the `.trx` in with that SHA leading its name, add a row above,
and write the `RUN.md` beside it. Do not overwrite an existing row — a superseded run is still the
honest record of what was true that day.

The other way a number here can measure the wrong thing is `--no-build` over a stale assembly: see the
`--no-build` note in the repo's `CLAUDE.md` under **Commands → Tests** before recording any run produced
with it.

## What a green suite here is not

It is evidence that code behaves. It is not evidence that a capability exists or is reachable, and it
is never acceptance — acceptance is a person completing the journey.
